--- Search.md ---
Segments | Space Lua and the command line
The same search is available from [[Space Lua]] as `search.status()` and `search.find()`. See [[API/search]].

From the command line, run it with `sb lua` (see [[CLI]]):

```bash
sb lua 'search.find("garden plan")'
sb lua 'search.find("flat tire", {mode = "phrase", limit = 10})'
```

The result is JSON with `items` (each with `page`, `line`, `excerpt` and more) and `total`.

--- Vim.md ---
Configuration | Configuration
Using Space Lua:

```lua
config.set {
  vim = {
    unmap = {
      "<Space>",
      {
        key = "<C-c>",
        mode = "insert",
      },
    },
    map = {
      {
        map = "jj",
        to = "<Esc>",
        mode = "insert",
      },
    },
    noremap = {
      {
        map = "<",
        to = "<gv",
        mode = "visual",
      },
      {
        map = "<Space>ff",
        to = ":findfile<CR>",
        mode = "normal",
      },
      {
        map = "<Space>rc",
        to = ":runcommand<CR>",
        mode = "normal",
      },
    },
    commands = {
      {
        command = "Navigate: Page Picker",
        ex = "findfile",
      },
      {
        command = "Open Command Palette",
        ex = "runcommand",
      },
    }
  }
}
```

--- CLI.md ---
Installation | Or pipe from stdin:
echo 'local x = 40; return x + 2' | sb lua

sb lua 'system.reboot()'       # after changing files outside sb (not needed after sb fs write/edit)
sb lua 'space.lint("Page")'    # problems on a page; space.lint() checks every page
```

To check a page without opening it in the editor, call [[API/space#space.lint]]: it runs the editor’s linters (frontmatter and data block YAML, `space-lua` syntax, objects checked against tag schemas, anchors) and renders every `${…}` directive and Lua code widget, returning a list of `{page, line, column, severity, message, source}` for the ones that fail. An empty list means no problems. A page written with `sb fs write` or `sb fs edit` (or through the [[MCP]]) has its scripts reloaded already, so `space.lint` sees them. Only after changing files by other means do you need `sb lua 'system.reboot()'` first.

--- Library/silverbullet-diagram/Mermaid.md ---
Installation | Configuration
Everything works out of the box, but you can override the defaults via Space Lua config:

```lua
config.set("mermaid", {
  -- ESM URL imported when no local bundle is installed (default: esm.sh).
  -- Also the URL the "Download bundle for offline use" command fetches from:
  cdnUrl = "https://esm.sh/mermaid@11.4.0?bundle",
  -- Space path the offline bundle is written to / loaded from:
  bundlePath = "Library/silverbullet-diagram/mermaid.bundle.js",
})
```

All keys are optional. By default diagrams load from `cdnUrl`. Once a bundle exists at `bundlePath` (e.g. after running **`Mermaid: Download bundle for offline use`**), the local copy is used automatically.

